      - "Week 1 starts Monday 29 December 2025; add 52 × 7 days"
    result: "Monday 28 December 2026 to Sunday 3 January 2027"
    link: "/tools/week-number-calculator/?wy=2026&wn=53#tool"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Assuming week 1 starts on January 1"
    description: "In ISO 8601, week 1 can start as early as 29 December or as late as 4 January. Days at the very start or end of a year often belong to the neighbouring year's week."
  - title: "Using the calendar year with the ISO week"
    description: "1 January 2027 is week 53 of 2026, not week 53 of 2027. Always write the ISO week-year with the week (2026-W53). In code, use the week-year format (<code>%G</code> with <code>%V</code> in strftime, or <code>IYYY</code> with <code>IW</code> in PostgreSQL and Oracle) rather than the calendar year."
  - title: "Mixing US and ISO week numbers"
    description: "US calendars and some spreadsheets start weeks on Sunday and count the week containing January 1 as week 1. The same date can then be one week apart in the two systems. Excel's <code>WEEKNUM</code> uses the US rule by default; <code>ISOWEEKNUM</code> uses ISO 8601."
  - title: "Assuming every year has 52 weeks"
    description: "52 × 7 = 364 days, so the leftover day or two add up to a 53rd ISO week every five or six years. 2020, 2026 and 2032 all have 53 weeks, which matters for weekly payroll, reporting and fiscal calendars."
  - title: "Calculating with local time near midnight"
    description: "If code computes weeks from a timestamp in UTC while the user is in another time zone, Sunday night or Monday morning can land in the wrong week. Convert to the user's local date first, then take the week number."
faqs:
  - q: "What week number is it today?"
    a: "The box at the top of the calculator shows today's ISO 8601 week number, using your device's date, along with its Monday and Sunday dates."
  - q: "How are week numbers calculated?"
    a: "Under ISO 8601, weeks run Monday to Sunday and week 1 is the week containing the first Thursday of the year. Each week belongs to the year in which its Thursday falls."
  - q: "Which years have 53 weeks?"
    a: "A year has 53 ISO weeks if 1 January is a Thursday, or if it is a leap year and 1 January is a Wednesday. Recent and upcoming examples are 2015, 2020, 2026, 2032 and 2037."
  - q: "Does the week start on Monday or Sunday?"
    a: "ISO 8601, used across Europe and most of Asia, starts weeks on Monday. In the US, Canada and some other countries, calendars usually start the week on Sunday. The calculator shows the ISO number and, labelled separately, the US-style number."
  - q: "What dates are in week 1 of 2026?"
    a: "ISO week 1 of 2026 runs from Monday 29 December 2025 to Sunday 4 January 2026."
  - q: "What is the difference between ISOWEEKNUM and WEEKNUM in Excel?"
    a: "ISOWEEKNUM returns the ISO 8601 week (Monday start, first-Thursday rule). WEEKNUM with its default return type counts weeks from Sunday, with week 1 being the week containing January 1, which matches the US-style number here."
  - q: "How do I write an ISO week date?"
    a: "Write the ISO week-year, W, the two-digit week number and optionally the weekday: 2026-W44 for the week or 2026-W44-6 for its Saturday. The compact form is 2026W446."
